  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Safiuddin Badruddin Khan vs State Of Maharashtra on 29 January, 2026 2026:Bhc-As:4473 Prasad Rajput (P.A.) 9_Ba_299_202 In The High Court Of Judicature At Bombay Criminal Appellate Jurisdiction Bail Application No.299 Of 2026 Safiuddin Badruddin Khan ...Appl Versus State of Maharashtra ... Resp Mr. Tuushar Sonawane, for the Applicant. Mr. Mayur S. Sonavane, App for the Respondent - State. Gpsi - Mohan Parab, Anc Crime Branch, Thane, present. Coram Dr. Neela Gokhale, J. Dated: 29Th January 2026 Pc:- 1.",
    "facts": "It is alleged that on the basis of secret information the police officials and its team intercepted a vehicle carrying Pan Masal and Tobacco, value of which being Rs.6,48,000/-. The Applicant herein is stated to be the driver of the vehicle. Pursuant to the FIR being registered, the Applicant was arrested on 23rd December, 2025. 3. The Applicant made an application seeking bail before the Additional Sessions Judge, Thane, however, by order dated 9th January, 2026, the said Application was rejected. Hence, the Applicant has filed the present Bail Application for the reliefs as prayed. The Applicant was arrested on 23rd December, 2025 and has suffered incarceration for 1 month and 3 days.",
    "arguments": "4. Mr. Tuushar Sonawane, learned counsel for the Applicant, submits that save and except the offence punishable under Section 123 of the BNS, all other offences are bailable. Moreover, this Court has already held that mere possession, transportation, storage or sale of Pan Masala packets would not attract Section 328 of the IPC (Section 123 of the BNS) and the said issue is now pending before the Supreme Court. Page 2 of 5 th 29 January 2026 ::: Uploaded on - 29/01/2026 ::: Downloaded on - 29/01/2026 20:52:16 6. Mr. Mayur Sonavane, learned APP representing the State, fairly concedes that the matter is pending before the Supreme Court.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4. Mr. Tuushar Sonawane, learned counsel for the Applicant, submits that save and except the offence punishable under Section 123 of the BNS, all other offences are bailable. Moreover, this Court has already held that mere possession, transportation, storage or sale of Pan Masala packets would not attract Section 328 of the IPC (Section 123 of the BNS) and the said issue is now pending before the Supreme Court. Page 2 of 5 th 29 January 2026 ::: Uploaded on - 29/01/2026 ::: Downloaded on - 29/01/2026 20:52:16",
    "respondent_arguments": "6. Mr. Mayur Sonavane, learned APP representing the State, fairly concedes that the matter is pending before the Supreme Court.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
